<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Notarization requires the hardened runtime, which denies executable memory
    by default. The sidecar is a compiled Bun binary, so JavaScriptCore JITs -
    without these two entitlements the kernel SIGKILLs it before it can
    announce its port (ADR-0002), and the app launches to a permanently empty
    window: every number in the UI comes from the sidecar.

    BOTH keys are required, and the second one is easy to miss. Granting only
    allow-jit is enough to run the sidecar by hand from a shell, but NOT enough
    when the app spawns it - that path still died with
    "EXC_BAD_ACCESS / SIGKILL (Code Signature Invalid), CODESIGNING Invalid
    Page" on a JIT page. So a green standalone probe does not prove the app
    works; only launching the built app does. Verified 2026-07-29:
    allow-jit alone => app-spawned sidecar killed;
    allow-jit + allow-unsigned-executable-memory => sidecar live and listening.
    disable-library-validation was tested and is NOT needed (the bun:ffi
    watchdog only dlopens Apple-signed libc).

    None of this reproduces in an unnotarized local build - `tauri dev` and
    `install_desktop.sh` produce ad-hoc-signed binaries with no hardened
    runtime, where the JIT is unrestricted. It appears only in a signed release
    build, and notarization still passes either way, so neither the release
    gate nor Apple will tell you it is broken.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
